nderstands general and detailed information as well as attitude and intention on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear"}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Understands general and detailed information as well as attitude and intention on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear"}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands general and detailed information as well as attitude and intention on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear"}]}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn9le15ob04re1img01.jpg"}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "infamous"}, {"type": "definition", "content": " famous for something which is considered bad"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The musician had some infamous parties."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le15ob04aud01"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to talk about jobs and fame."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "notorious"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "known for something bad"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: He's notorious for missing concerts."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le15ob04aud02"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "bad press"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "when negative things are published about someone or something in a newspaper or on television"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: He received a lot of bad press after the scandal. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le15ob04aud03"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "media"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "television, internet, newspapers, magazines and radio combined as a group "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Has the media been tough on you?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le15ob04aud04"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "sensationalized"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "embellished in order to make more exciting but less accurate "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: They sensationalized the story."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le15ob04aud05"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "press"}, {"type": "definition", "content": " those who report or publish the news (newspapers, television, radio, etc.)"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: As a blogger, I am also part of the press."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le15ob04aud06"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "publicist"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "someone responsible for increasing the popularity of a person or company"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I really need to fire my publicist."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le15ob04aud07"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I write a list of questions to ask a famous celebrity."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I conduct an interview with a celebrity. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I provide feedback using stress."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I respond to interview questions."}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Pretend you run your own blog and you are about to interview your favorite celebrity. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Choose a celebrity to interview. It can be anyone, living or dead."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write a minimum of ten questions to ask."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Role play the interview with your partner playing the part of the celebrity."}, {"type": "text", "content": "If they answer any of the questions incorrectly, correct them by stressing the appropriate answer."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Reverse the roles and try again."}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Tonic, Contrastive, Emphatic and New Information Stress"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "There are various types of stress used when speaking English."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Tonic stress</span> refers to the syllable in a word which receives the most stress in an intonation unit. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">Well, let's go with <span class=\"strong\">no</span>table. </span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you want to point out the difference between one object and another, you use <span class=\"strong\">contrastive stress</span>. It tends to be used with determiners such as 'this, that, these and those'."}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example: </br><span class=\"italic\">Anyway, it was a <span class=\"strong\">two</span>-day bender, not <span class=\"strong\">three</span>.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">To emphasize</span> a word, you can change the stress from the principal word to another content word. By choosing a word other than the one you normally stress, you put emphasis on the new one."}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example:<br/><span class=\"italic\">You can call me Ms. Davis or Sally, but definitely <span class=\"strong\">not</span> baby.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Sentence stress gives English its <SPAN CLASS=\"STRONG\"> rhythm </span> or \"beat\". "}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you<span class=\"strong\"> stress new information</span>, you pronounce it with more breath force, since it is more prominent. "}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Reduction of Have in Past Modals and of Auxiliary Verbs"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When you use auxiliary verbs like have, it is common to reduce it in conversation. </br></br>  In English, not every word or syllable is pronounced fully."}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you reduce the sound of have it sounds like <span class=\"strong\">uv</span> or <span class=\"strong\">iv.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Read each sentence in it's full form:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "I <span class=\"strong\">could have</span> just skipped it, like I did that concert.</br> It sounds like your bad attitude <span class=\"strong\">might have</span> been a factor in their reporting. </br>I <span class=\"strong\">must have</span> got my schedule mixed up. </br>I <span class=\"strong\">would have</span> been more careful."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Now read them and reduce the pronunciation of the auxiliary verb have:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "I <span class=\"strong\">could've</span> just skipped it, like I did that concert.  could_uv</br> It sounds like your bad attitude <span class=\"strong\">might've</span> been a factor in their reporting. might_uv</br> I <span class=\"strong\">must've</span> got my schedule mixed up. must_uv</br> I <span class=\"strong\">would've</span> been more careful. would_uv"}]}], "labs": [{"exercises": [{"template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [0], "type": "WLC", "questions": [], "img_path": "", "id": 0, "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can talk about fame and work."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can use different tones to stress, contrast or emphasize i